<?php

return [
    'everest' => [
        'name' => 'Everest',
        'slug' => 'everest',
        'region_label' => 'Khumbu & Sagarmatha National Park',

        'summary' => 'The Everest region is Nepal\'s most iconic high-Himalayan trekking destination, home to Mount Everest, Lhotse, Nuptse and the unmistakable pyramid of Ama Dablam. Ancient Sherpa settlements, Buddhist monasteries, glacier-fed valleys and legendary high-altitude trails make Khumbu both a cultural journey and one of the world\'s great mountain adventures.',

        'description' => '<p>The Everest region, traditionally known as Khumbu, occupies the upper valleys of northeastern Nepal within the Mahalangur Himal. Its landscapes rise from forested river corridors around 2,600 metres to the highest mountain terrain on Earth, crowned by Mount Everest at 8,848.86 metres. Lhotse, Nuptse, Cho Oyu, Ama Dablam, Thamserku and Kangtega dominate the surrounding skyline, while the Dudh Koshi and Imja Khola drain some of the Himalaya\'s best-known glacial systems. The Khumbu Glacier, Ngozumpa Glacier and Imja basin shape the high valleys, and classic trekking routes connect settlements and viewpoints through high passes including Cho La, Renjo La and Kongma La.</p><p>Khumbu is the historic homeland of the Sherpa people, whose Tibetan Buddhist heritage is woven into the landscape. Villages such as Namche Bazaar, Khumjung, Khunde, Pangboche, Phortse and Thame are marked by mani walls, chortens, prayer wheels and family-run lodges. Tengboche Monastery is the region\'s best-known religious centre, while monasteries at Pangboche and Thame remain important to local spiritual life. Seasonal festivals, mountain deities and pilgrimage traditions reflect a living culture in which Buddhism, trade, pastoralism and mountaineering have evolved together for generations.</p><p>The trail environment changes dramatically with altitude. Lower sections pass through pine, fir, birch and rhododendron forest before opening into juniper scrub, alpine meadow, moraine and glacial terrain. Sagarmatha National Park protects Himalayan tahr, musk deer, Himalayan monal and other high-mountain species, with snow leopard and red panda surviving in remote habitats. The Everest region also has Nepal\'s most developed high-altitude teahouse network, allowing trekkers to travel without camping on the main routes. Spring brings flowering forests and expedition activity, autumn offers the clearest skies and most stable trekking weather, and winter rewards experienced visitors with quieter trails and exceptionally crisp mountain views.</p>',

        'gateway' => 'Lukla and the wider Solukhumbu road network are the principal gateways to the Everest region. Most classic itineraries use a mountain flight to Tenzing-Hillary Airport at Lukla, commonly from Kathmandu during quieter periods and from Ramechhap/Manthali during busy trekking seasons. Kathmandu to Manthali generally requires about 4-5 hours by road, followed by an approximately 15-20 minute mountain flight to Lukla. Overland approaches are possible through Salleri, Phaplu and lower Solukhumbu, normally requiring around 8-10 hours or more of road travel from Kathmandu before continuing toward Surke, Lukla or the Dudh Koshi valley.',

        'trailheads' => 'Lukla is the principal trailhead for Everest Base Camp, Gokyo, Everest View and Three Passes itineraries. Surke is used when road access or flight arrangements favour a lower start. Longer lower-Solukhumbu approaches may begin from Phaplu, Salleri or nearby roadheads and join the main Khumbu trail gradually. Jiri is the historic trailhead associated with early Everest expeditions, although modern road access has shifted most contemporary trekking starts farther east.',

        'permits' => 'Everest-region trekking requires the permits and registrations applicable to the chosen route. Standard Khumbu itineraries require Sagarmatha National Park entry and the local Khumbu Pasang Lhamu Rural Municipality visitor fee. Nepal Tourism Board currently lists Everest Base Camp, Gokyo, Everest View, Cho La, Renjo La and Three Passes among routes subject to the revised TIMS and licensed-guide framework. Standard Lukla-Namche-Everest Base Camp and Gokyo routes are not Department of Immigration restricted-area routes. Permit names, fees and local collection procedures can change by fiscal year, so all rates and requirements should be reconfirmed immediately before departure.',

        'pacing_note' => 'High-altitude pacing is critical in the Everest region. Namche Bazaar at approximately 3,440 metres should normally serve as the first major acclimatization stop, with an additional acclimatization day around Dingboche or a similar elevation before sleeping substantially above 4,500 metres. Above roughly 3,000 metres, a conservative guideline is to limit sleeping-elevation gain to about 300-500 metres per day and include a rest or acclimatization day after approximately every 1,000 metres gained. Walk slowly, hydrate consistently and avoid rapid ascents created by compressed itineraries. Do not ascend to sleep higher when altitude symptoms are worsening. Severe headache, repeated vomiting, confusion, ataxia, breathlessness at rest or rapidly deteriorating condition requires immediate descent and medical or rescue assessment.',

        'meta_title' => 'Everest Region Treks | Khumbu & Everest Base Camp',

        'meta_description' => 'Explore Nepal\'s Everest region, from Sherpa villages and Tengboche to Gokyo, high passes and Everest Base Camp beneath the world\'s highest peaks.',
    ],

    'annapurna' => [
        'name' => 'Annapurna',
        'slug' => 'annapurna',
        'region_label' => 'Annapurna Conservation Area & Gurung Highlands',

        'summary' => 'Annapurna is Nepal\'s most diverse trekking region, combining subtropical valleys, Gurung villages, rhododendron forests, deep river gorges and high Himalayan desert. From Annapurna Base Camp to Thorong La and the Kali Gandaki, the region offers an extraordinary range of landscapes within a single mountain system.',

        'description' => '<p>The Annapurna region surrounds the vast Annapurna massif of central Nepal and contains one of the Himalaya\'s greatest variations in elevation, climate and terrain. Annapurna I rises to 8,091 metres, accompanied by Annapurna II, III and IV, Annapurna South, Hiunchuli and the sacred summit of Machhapuchhre. Across the Kali Gandaki stands Dhaulagiri at 8,167 metres, creating one of the world\'s most dramatic high-mountain corridors. Trekking routes move from river valleys below 1,000 metres to the enclosed Annapurna Sanctuary, the high plateau of Manang and Thorong La at 5,416 metres, linking subtropical foothills with alpine basins, glacial moraine and trans-Himalayan desert.</p><p>The southern Annapurna foothills are closely associated with Gurung communities, particularly around Ghandruk, Landruk, Sikles and the Modi Khola valley, while Magar communities are prominent farther west. North of the main Himalayan crest, Manang, Braga, Ngawal, Pisang and Mustang-influenced settlements show a stronger Tibetan Buddhist cultural character. Monasteries, chortens, mani walls and traditional stone architecture remain important throughout the upper valleys. Muktinath is one of Nepal\'s most important pilgrimage destinations, sacred to both Hindus and Buddhists, while Braga Monastery and smaller village gompas preserve centuries of Himalayan religious tradition.</p><p>The Annapurna Conservation Area protects ecosystems ranging from subtropical broadleaf forest and bamboo to oak, rhododendron, conifer woodland, alpine meadow and arid highland. Himalayan tahr, blue sheep, musk deer and snow leopard occur in the wider conservation landscape, while pheasants and raptors are common across forest and alpine zones. Annapurna has one of Nepal\'s most extensive teahouse networks, supporting everything from short foothill walks to multi-week circuits. March and April are especially attractive for rhododendron bloom, October and November usually provide the clearest views, and the northern rain-shadow valleys around Manang and Mustang can remain comparatively dry even when southern slopes receive heavier monsoon rainfall.</p>',

        'gateway' => 'Pokhara is the main gateway to the Annapurna region, approximately 200 kilometres west of Kathmandu. Travel from Kathmandu typically takes around 7-9 hours by road depending on traffic and road conditions, while scheduled domestic flights take about 25-30 minutes. From Pokhara, roads provide access toward Nayapul, Birethanti, Ghandruk, Jhinu Danda, Kande, Phedi and Mardi Himal approaches. Annapurna Circuit itineraries generally approach through Besisahar and the Marsyangdi valley, with road access now extending toward Dharapani, Chame and other upper-valley settlements depending on seasonal conditions. Jomsom Airport provides an additional weather-dependent air gateway for Kali Gandaki and Lower Mustang itineraries.',

        'trailheads' => 'Common southern trailheads include Birethanti, Nayapul, Ghandruk-area roadheads, Jhinu Danda, Kande and Phedi. Annapurna Base Camp itineraries often begin from Birethanti, Ghandruk or Jhinu depending on available road access. Annapurna Circuit treks traditionally start around Besisahar or Bhulbhule, although many modern itineraries begin farther up the Marsyangdi corridor at Jagat, Dharapani or Chame. Jomsom, Marpha and Muktinath serve as important entry or exit points on the western side of the massif.',

        'permits' => 'Most Annapurna itineraries require an Annapurna Conservation Area Permit (ACAP). Nepal Tourism Board currently lists major routes including the Annapurna Circuit, Poon Hill-ABC, Khopra and Mardi Himal under the revised TIMS and licensed-guide framework. Standard Annapurna Base Camp and conventional Annapurna Circuit routes are not Department of Immigration restricted areas. Extensions into Nar-Phu or other officially restricted zones require the applicable Restricted Area Permit in addition to conservation-area and TIMS documentation. Permit fees and procedures should be reconfirmed before departure because official rates can change.',

        'pacing_note' => 'Lower Annapurna itineraries allow relatively flexible pacing, but the upper Marsyangdi valley, Tilicho area and Thorong La require disciplined acclimatization. Avoid travelling rapidly by vehicle from low elevation directly into the upper valley without sufficient adaptation. Manang at approximately 3,540 metres is the principal acclimatization base for most circuit itineraries. Above 3,000 metres, sleeping elevation should generally increase by no more than about 300-500 metres per day, with an additional acclimatization day after roughly every 1,000 metres gained. Thorong La should only be attempted by well-acclimatized trekkers in suitable weather and trail conditions. Snow, wind, avalanche exposure and altitude illness must all be considered before moving above Thorong Phedi or High Camp.',

        'meta_title' => 'Annapurna Treks | Base Camp, Circuit & Himalaya',

        'meta_description' => 'Discover Annapurna Base Camp, the Annapurna Circuit, Gurung villages, rhododendron forests and dramatic high passes across central Nepal.',
    ],

    'langtang' => [
        'name' => 'Langtang',
        'slug' => 'langtang',
        'region_label' => 'Langtang National Park & Tamang Himal',

        'summary' => 'Langtang is the nearest major Himalayan trekking region to Kathmandu, yet its glacier-carved valleys and high alpine landscapes feel remarkably remote. Tamang villages, Buddhist monasteries, sacred lakes and the great wall of Langtang Lirung give the region a distinctive cultural and mountain identity.',

        'description' => '<p>Langtang lies immediately north of Kathmandu along Nepal\'s border with the Tibetan Plateau. The main valley follows the Langtang Khola through a steep glacial trough beneath Langtang Lirung, which rises to 7,227 metres. Dorje Lakpa, Yala Peak, Langshisa Ri and the surrounding Himalayan ranges frame the upper valley, while trekking viewpoints above Kyanjin reach well beyond 4,500 metres. To the southwest, the Gosainkunda basin contains a chain of sacred alpine lakes beneath rugged ridges and passes including Laurebina La, creating a landscape that changes rapidly from deep forest to exposed high-alpine terrain.</p><p>The region is closely associated with Tamang communities whose language, architecture, dress and Buddhist traditions reflect long-standing cultural exchange across the Himalaya. Syabrubesi, Gatlang, Thuman, Briddim and other villages preserve important elements of Tamang cultural life, while Langtang Village has become a powerful symbol of resilience following the 2015 earthquake and avalanche disaster. Kyanjin Gompa is the best-known monastery settlement in the upper valley, and prayer walls, chortens, mani stones and village gompas remain integral to the cultural landscape.</p><p>Langtang National Park protects extensive oak, maple, pine, fir, birch and rhododendron forest before the vegetation gives way to juniper scrub and open alpine pasture. The park is particularly important for red panda habitat and also supports Himalayan black bear, musk deer, Himalayan tahr, ghoral and diverse mountain birdlife. Teahouse accommodation is well established throughout the Langtang Valley, Gosainkunda and Tamang Heritage corridors. Spring is attractive for flowering forests, autumn usually provides the clearest weather, and winter can be rewarding for experienced trekkers, although snow may complicate high passes and upper-valley excursions.</p>',

        'gateway' => 'Syabrubesi and Dhunche in Rasuwa District are the main overland gateways. Both are reached from Kathmandu through Trishuli and the mountain highway north toward Rasuwa. Practical travel time is usually around 6-8 hours to Dhunche and 7-9 hours to Syabrubesi, depending on traffic, road maintenance, monsoon damage and seasonal conditions. No domestic flight is normally required for the standard Langtang Valley trek, making Langtang one of Nepal\'s most accessible major Himalayan regions from Kathmandu.',

        'trailheads' => 'Syabrubesi is the principal trailhead for Langtang Valley and many Tamang Heritage itineraries. Dhunche is commonly used for Gosainkunda approaches, while Thulo Syabru links the Langtang, Gosainkunda and Helambu trail systems. Gatlang and nearby villages are frequently used as starting points for Tamang Heritage routes. Sundarijal on the northeastern edge of Kathmandu Valley can also serve as the starting point for Helambu and Gosainkunda itineraries approached from the south.',

        'permits' => 'Langtang itineraries require Langtang National Park entry. Nepal Tourism Board currently lists Langtang Trek, Langtang-Gosainkunda, Tamang Heritage and Ganja La among routes subject to the revised TIMS and licensed-guide framework. Standard Langtang Valley, Gosainkunda and Tamang Heritage itineraries are not Department of Immigration restricted-area routes. Any itinerary approaching sensitive border zones or officially restricted corridors must comply with additional local or immigration requirements. Permit fees and procedures should always be checked immediately before departure.',

        'pacing_note' => 'Langtang gains altitude quickly despite its short travel distance from Kathmandu. A sensible progression is to move gradually through the lower valley before sleeping at Langtang Village and then Kyanjin Gompa around 3,870 metres. Use Kyanjin as an acclimatization base before attempting high viewpoints such as Kyanjin Ri or Tserko Ri. Above approximately 3,000 metres, limit sleeping-elevation gain to roughly 300-500 metres per day whenever possible. Gosainkunda itineraries also require careful staging because overnight elevations rise above 4,000 metres. High viewpoint days should be treated as climb-high, sleep-lower excursions, and worsening symptoms of altitude illness should always trigger descent rather than continued ascent.',

        'meta_title' => 'Langtang Treks | Valley, Gosainkunda & Tamang',

        'meta_description' => 'Trek Langtang Valley, Gosainkunda and Tamang villages through red panda habitat, alpine pasture and dramatic Himalayan scenery near Kathmandu.',
    ],

    'manaslu' => [
        'name' => 'Manaslu',
        'slug' => 'manaslu',
        'region_label' => 'Manaslu Conservation Area & Nubri Highlands',

        'summary' => 'Manaslu offers one of Nepal\'s finest remote circuits, following the Budhi Gandaki from subtropical gorges into the Tibetan-influenced highlands beneath 8,163-metre Manaslu. The journey combines exceptional cultural depth, wild mountain scenery and the demanding crossing of Larkya La.',

        'description' => '<p>The Manaslu region surrounds Mount Manaslu, the world\'s eighth-highest mountain at 8,163 metres, in the Gorkha Himal of west-central Nepal. The classic circuit follows the Budhi Gandaki upstream through deep river gorges before opening into the high valleys of Nubri. Himalchuli, Ngadi Chuli, Shringi Himal and the broader Ganesh Himal ranges contribute to an exceptionally dramatic skyline, while glaciers descend from the Manaslu massif above Samagaun and Samdo. The route reaches its highest trekking point at Larkya La, approximately 5,106 metres, before descending toward Bhimtang and the upper Marsyangdi valley.</p><p>The cultural transition from lower Gorkha into upper Nubri is one of the defining features of the trek. Gurung and other hill communities occupy the lower valleys, while villages such as Lho, Shyala, Samagaun and Samdo show strong Tibetan Buddhist cultural influence. Ribung Gompa at Lho, Pungyen Gompa beneath Manaslu and monasteries around Samagaun remain important centres of religious life. Mani walls, chortens, kani gateways and stone-built settlements reflect centuries of trade and cultural contact across the Himalayan frontier.</p><p>The Manaslu Conservation Area protects ecosystems ranging from subtropical forest and temperate woodland to rhododendron, conifer forest, alpine meadow and glacial terrain. Wildlife recorded in the wider region includes snow leopard, Himalayan tahr, musk deer and blue sheep. Teahouse infrastructure has improved considerably, but the circuit remains quieter and more remote than the major Everest and Annapurna routes. Autumn is the classic season for stable weather and clear pass conditions, while spring brings warmer temperatures and flowering forests. Winter snow may block Larkya La, and the monsoon can make the lower Budhi Gandaki corridor wet and landslide-prone.</p>',

        'gateway' => 'The Manaslu Circuit is reached overland from Kathmandu through the Gorkha and Dhading road networks toward the Budhi Gandaki valley. Current road access commonly allows vehicles to reach Machha Khola or nearby roadheads, although travel time and the final accessible point vary significantly with season and road condition. Allow roughly 7-10 hours or more from Kathmandu for the approach. After crossing Larkya La, trekkers exit through Bhimtang and Dharapani, where the route joins the Annapurna road corridor toward Besisahar and onward to Kathmandu or Pokhara.',

        'trailheads' => 'Machha Khola is now the most common practical starting point for the Manaslu Circuit. Soti Khola and Arughat remain important historic approach points and may still be used depending on road access and itinerary design. The standard route continues through Jagat, Deng, Namrung, Lho, Samagaun and Samdo before crossing Larkya La. Dharapani is the principal western exit and also allows connection with the Annapurna Circuit. Tsum Valley itineraries branch from the main Budhi Gandaki corridor around the Lokpa/Philim area.',

        'permits' => 'The upper Manaslu corridor is a Department of Immigration Restricted Area and requires a Manaslu Restricted Area Permit arranged through a Government-registered trekking agency. The Department of Immigration currently lists the fee at USD 100 per person for the first week plus USD 15 per additional day from September-November, and USD 75 per person for the first week plus USD 10 per additional day from December-August. Trekkers also require Manaslu Conservation Area entry, and standard circuit itineraries continue into the Annapurna Conservation Area, so ACAP is normally required as well. Nepal Tourism Board lists Manaslu Circuit and Manaslu-Tsum Valley under the revised TIMS and licensed-guide framework. Restricted-area group, guide and permit procedures must be confirmed before booking because regulations are actively enforced.',

        'pacing_note' => 'Manaslu requires conservative acclimatization because the route rises from low subtropical elevations to villages above 3,500 metres before crossing Larkya La at approximately 5,106 metres. Do not compress the upper itinerary to compensate for fast progress lower in the valley. Samagaun at around 3,530 metres should normally be used as the principal acclimatization base, with a climb-high excursion toward Pungyen Gompa, Birendra Tal or the Manaslu Base Camp direction when conditions allow. Samdo provides further adaptation before Dharamsala and the pass. Above 3,000 metres, keep sleeping-elevation gains generally within 300-500 metres per day. Larkya La should only be attempted when acclimatization, weather, snow conditions and group health are all satisfactory.',

        'meta_title' => 'Manaslu Circuit Trek | Remote Himalayan Wilderness',

        'meta_description' => 'Explore the Manaslu Circuit through Nubri villages, Buddhist monasteries and the remote Budhi Gandaki valley before crossing high Larkya La.',
    ],

    'mustang' => [
        'name' => 'Mustang',
        'slug' => 'mustang',
        'region_label' => 'Upper Mustang & the Trans-Himalayan Kingdom of Lo',

        'summary' => 'Mustang is Nepal\'s great trans-Himalayan desert, a landscape of wind-carved cliffs, ancient cave complexes, fortified villages and Tibetan Buddhist monasteries beyond the Annapurna-Dhaulagiri rain shadow. Upper Mustang leads to the walled settlement of Lo Manthang through one of Nepal\'s most culturally distinctive restricted trekking regions.',

        'description' => '<p>Mustang occupies the upper Kali Gandaki valley between the Annapurna and Dhaulagiri massifs and extends north toward the Tibetan Plateau. Sheltered behind the main Himalayan crest, it lies in a pronounced rain shadow where wind and erosion have sculpted red, ochre and grey cliffs into deeply fluted canyons and barren high plateaus. Elevations along the main Upper Mustang route generally range from around 2,700 metres near Jomsom to settlements and passes between approximately 3,500 and 4,200 metres. Nilgiri, Annapurna, Dhaulagiri and Tukuche Peak dominate the southern approaches, while the Kali Gandaki cuts through one of the Himalaya\'s most striking geological corridors.</p><p>Upper Mustang preserves the heritage of the former Kingdom of Lo and remains culturally closely connected to the Tibetan Buddhist world. Lo Manthang is enclosed by historic walls and contains important monasteries including Jampa, Thubchen and Chode Gompa. Villages such as Tsarang, Ghami, Samar and Dhakmar contain mani walls, chortens, traditional mud-brick architecture and centuries-old religious art. Ancient cliff-cave systems around Chhoser and elsewhere reveal long histories of habitation, meditation, burial and trade. The annual Tiji Festival remains one of the region\'s most distinctive living religious traditions, combining masked dances, ritual performance and monastery ceremony.</p><p>Mustang\'s trail environment is unlike Nepal\'s forested trekking regions. Routes cross exposed plateaus, dry riverbeds, eroded gullies and windswept passes, with willow, poplar, buckwheat and barley concentrated around irrigated settlements. Blue sheep and Himalayan raptors inhabit the wider landscape, and the region forms part of important high trans-Himalayan snow leopard habitat. Teahouses are established along the main route, although facilities are simpler away from larger villages. Spring and autumn remain the principal trekking seasons, while the rain-shadow climate means Upper Mustang can also be a viable summer destination even when southern Nepal is experiencing monsoon rainfall.</p>',

        'gateway' => 'Pokhara is the principal staging city for Mustang. From Pokhara, Jomsom can be reached by a short weather-dependent mountain flight, typically around 20-25 minutes, or by a long overland journey through Beni, Tatopani, Ghasa, Marpha and the Kali Gandaki corridor. Road travel from Pokhara to Jomsom commonly requires roughly 8-12 hours depending on weather and road conditions. Upper Mustang journeys continue north through Kagbeni, Chele, Ghami and Tsarang toward Lo Manthang by trekking trail and increasingly by rough mountain road. Kathmandu to Pokhara generally takes around 7-9 hours by road or approximately 25-30 minutes by scheduled domestic flight.',

        'trailheads' => 'Jomsom is the principal aviation and road-access hub for Mustang. Kagbeni, at the confluence of the Kali Gandaki and Jhong rivers, is the traditional gateway to the restricted Upper Mustang corridor and the most common practical start for trekking north. Some itineraries begin walking from Jomsom, while others transfer directly to Kagbeni before entering the restricted area. Muktinath, Marpha and Tukuche are important trailheads for Lower Mustang routes, and Lo Manthang serves as the base for excursions toward Chhoser, Nyphu, Garphu and other northern settlements.',

        'permits' => 'Upper Mustang is an officially Restricted Area and requires a Department of Immigration Restricted Area Trekking Permit arranged through a Government-registered trekking agency. The Department of Immigration currently lists Upper Mustang at USD 50 per person per day for the designated restricted areas. Trekkers also require Annapurna Conservation Area entry, and Nepal Tourism Board lists Upper Mustang under the revised TIMS and licensed-guide framework. Lower Mustang itineraries that remain outside the designated Upper Mustang restricted boundary do not require the Upper Mustang Restricted Area Permit, but applicable conservation-area and TIMS requirements still apply. Because restricted-area boundaries, fees and procedures are regulatory matters, they should be reconfirmed immediately before booking and permit processing.',

        'pacing_note' => 'Upper Mustang is lower than the highest Everest or Manaslu itineraries, but several nights are spent above 3,500 metres and daily routes repeatedly cross passes around or above 4,000 metres. Allow gradual adaptation through Jomsom, Kagbeni and intermediate settlements rather than moving directly from low elevation to the upper plateau. Above approximately 3,000 metres, keep sleeping-elevation gains moderate and schedule easier days if anyone develops headache, nausea, unusual fatigue or disturbed sleep. Strong afternoon winds, dehydration, intense ultraviolet exposure and dry air are major operational considerations in Mustang. Start early, carry adequate water, use sun and wind protection, and descend rather than continuing upward if altitude symptoms are worsening.',

        'meta_title' => 'Upper Mustang Trek | Lo Manthang & Kingdom of Lo',

        'meta_description' => 'Journey through Upper Mustang to Lo Manthang, exploring Tibetan Buddhist monasteries, ancient caves and Nepal\'s spectacular trans-Himalayan desert.',
    ],
];
